### =========================================================================
###  The MCP server, by hand
###
###  Same tools the agent uses, mounted at /mcp so any MCP client can call
###  them. Normally Claude Desktop or Claude Code speaks this for you — doing
###  it manually once makes it obvious there is no magic in the protocol.
###
###  Two things make MCP different from the plain REST calls in api.http:
###
###    1. Every response comes back as Server-Sent Events, so the body reads
###       `event: message` / `data: {...}` rather than bare JSON. That's why
###       Accept has to list text/event-stream.
###    2. It is stateful. `initialize` hands back an `mcp-session-id` header,
###       and every later call has to send it back. Run these top to bottom.
###
###  This file is us as the SERVER. The agent is also an MCP *client* — it
###  calls tavily_search on Tavily's hosted server the same way any client
###  calls ours. See app/agent/research.py.
### =========================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000
# @baseUrl = https://your-service.onrender.com


### 1. Open a session. The response headers carry the session id.
# @name initialize
POST {{baseUrl}}/mcp/
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "initialize",
  "params": {
    "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
    "capabilities": {},
    "clientInfo": { "name": "rest-client", "version": "1" }
  }
}


### 2. Confirm you're ready. Returns 202 with an empty body — that's success.
#
# A notification has no "id" because nothing is expected back. Skipping this
# step leaves the session half-open and the calls below fail.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mcp/
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
mcp-session-id: {{initialize.response.headers.mcp-session-id}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "method": "notifications/initialized"
}


### 3. What tools does this server have?
#
# The descriptions and argument schemas here are generated from the docstrings
# and type hints in `../tools/server.py` — that's the whole reason those
# docstrings are written for a reader who isn't you. This is what a model sees
# when it decides which tool to reach for.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mcp/
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
mcp-session-id: {{initialize.response.headers.mcp-session-id}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 2,
  "method": "tools/list"
}


### 4. Call wiki_search — the tool the agent uses to find page titles.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mcp/
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
mcp-session-id: {{initialize.response.headers.mcp-session-id}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 3,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "wiki_search",
    "arguments": { "query": "photovoltaic effect", "limit": 3 }
  }
}


### 5. Call wiki_read with one of those titles.
#
# Swap the title for something wiki_search actually returned above. A title
# with no usable summary comes back null rather than erroring — try a
# different one instead of retrying the same.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mcp/
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
mcp-session-id: {{initialize.response.headers.mcp-session-id}}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 4,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "wiki_read",
    "arguments": { "title": "Photovoltaic effect" }
  }
}
